{"qid":"Q38","names":["Republic of Italy"],"title":"Republic of Italy","endonym":{"native":"Italia","lang":"it"},"capsule":"The Italian Republic (Repubblica Italiana) was a Napoleonic client state in northern Italy, proclaimed on 26 January 1802 from the reorganization of the Cisalpine Republic, with Napoleon Bonaparte as president and Francesco Melzi d'Eril as vice-president and effective head of government. Its territory encompassed Lombardy, Emilia, and the Romagna, with Milan as capital, and it introduced a civil code modeled on French precedents along with conscription, standardized weights and measures, and a reformed judiciary. Napoleon transformed it into the Kingdom of Italy on 17 March 1805, crowning himself king in Milan Cathedral on 26 May 1805, absorbing it into the broader imperial reorganization of the peninsula. The republic's brief existence nonetheless established administrative and legal precedents that shaped Italian unification movements decades later.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The boundaries of the Napoleonic Italian Republic are well attested from French administrative records and contemporary maps; territorial changes during the period are precisely documented.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Italian Republic (Napoleonic)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_Republic_(Napoleonic)"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Italy (Napoleonic)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Italy_(Napoleonic)"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Francesco Melzi d'Eril","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francesco_Melzi_d%27Eril"}],"britannica":"place/Italy","cowCode":null